Before deciding how to merge, you need to know whether two writes were truly concurrent (neither caused the other) or sequential (one happened after seeing the other). A vector clock attaches a per-replica counter to each write, and comparing two clocks tells you which case you're in:

```
write_A: {replica1: 2, replica2: 0}
write_B: {replica1: 1, replica2: 1}
# neither vector dominates the other -> truly concurrent, real conflict

write_C: {replica1: 3, replica2: 1}
# dominates write_B -> C happened after B, no conflict, C simply wins
```

Skipping this check and merging (or LWW-ing) everything as if it were concurrent risks discarding a write that was actually a deliberate, later correction.
